      "text": "To find the sum of the positive divisors of 400, we first need to determine its prime factorization. The number 400 can be factored as follows:\n\n\\[ 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\]\n\nThe formula for the sum of the divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k}) \\]\n\nApplying this formula to our number \\( 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\), we get:\n\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2) \\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[ 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31 \\]\n\\[ 1 + 5 + 25 = 31 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the divisors of 400 is:\n\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961 \\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the prime factorization of 961. We find:\n\n\\[ 961 = 31^2 \\]\n\nThe number 961 is a perfect square of 31, and its only prime factor is 31. The distinct prime factor of 961 is 31. Thus, the number of distinct prime factors of 961 is 1.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of 400 is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
