      "text": "To find the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of 400, we first need to determine the sum of the divisors of 400. The prime factorization of 400 is:\n\\[ 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\]\nThe sum of the divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is given by:\n\\[ \\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k}) \\]\nFor \\( 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2) \\]\nCalculating each part, we get:\n\\[ 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31 \\]\n\\[ 1 + 5 + 25 = 31 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ \\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961 \\]\nNext, we need to find the prime factorization of 961. We check if 961 is a perfect square:\n\\[ 961 = 31^2 \\]\nThe prime factorization of 961 is \\( 31^2 \\). Since 961 has only one distinct prime factor, the number of distinct prime factors of 961 is 1. Therefore, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of 400 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
